  "analysis": "Regime read: the portfolio is in a mild drawdown (-2.95% from peak, no drawdown actions triggered), and the recent data show broad 20-day weakness across most ETFs (A10 -5.5%, A08 -6.1%, A06 -3.5%, A09 bonds -4.3%, A05 gold -7.0%) with tech the exception (A11 +4.3%, A01 +7.2%). Notably both bonds and equities have fallen together, which points to a rate-driven selloff rather than a flight to safety — so the current 17.3% bond overweight is providing less hedge value than its label suggests. On the forecasts: medians carry no directional skill, so I ignore them; the widths are the reliable signal — A02 (4.0%), A09 (4.3%) and A04 (4.9%) are the most certain assets, while the single stocks (widths 7.6%-11.0%) are the least. The current portfolio sits well off baseline: bonds +8.3pp, A02 +4.4pp, A06 +4.3pp, while gold, emerging, energy and all four zero-weight stocks are below baseline. My plan is a partial normalization toward baseline, not a full one: trim the bond and broad-equity concentration (funding ~8pp), add a gold hedge and restore below-baseline diversifiers (emerging, energy, financials stock, and small single-stock positions), and deploy ~2.8pp of cash while keeping it just above the 10% floor. Total defensive (bonds+gold+cash) goes from ~32% to ~26%, still above baseline's ~19%, which is appropriate for a -2.95% drawdown with no forced de-risking. All targets respect the +15pp-above-baseline increase cap, the 25%/10%/30% caps, and sum to 1 with cash >= 10%.",
  "positions": [
    {
      "symbol": "A02",
      "target_weight": 0.10,
      "rationale": "Trim from 14.1% toward baseline (9.65%). Narrowest 5d interval (4.0%) keeps it the largest equity holding, but 14% in one ETF is concentration; proceeds fund diversification. Stays +0.35pp above baseline.",
      "confidence": 0.53
    },
    {
      "symbol": "A11",
      "target_weight": 0.0817,
      "rationale": "Unchanged. Tech ETF with the strongest recent momentum (+4.3% 20d); moderate width (6.1%). Retained +1.76pp above baseline; tech group total 11.2% vs 30% cap.",
      "confidence": 0.5
    },
    {
      "symbol": "A10",
      "target_weight": 0.0646,
      "rationale": "Unchanged, essentially at baseline (6.55%). 20d return -5.5% argues against adding despite moderate width (5.9%).",
      "confidence": 0.5
    },
    {
      "symbol": "A06",
      "target_weight": 0.10,
      "rationale": "Trim from 12.5% to 10%. Second-narrowest interval (4.8%) supports keeping it well above baseline (8.16%), but -3.5% over 20d and single-ETF concentration justify the trim.",
      "confidence": 0.53
    },
    {
      "symbol": "A12",
      "target_weight": 0.045,
      "rationale": "Add from 2.3% back to baseline (4.6%). Widest ETF interval (8.4%) and 21% vol keep it at/below baseline; small diversifier, not a conviction add.",
      "confidence": 0.52
    },
    {
      "symbol": "A03",
      "target_weight": 0.05,
      "rationale": "Add from 3.1% toward baseline (5.25%). Energy vol 20.7% and width 7.4% justify staying below baseline.",
      "confidence": 0.52
    },
    {
      "symbol": "A04",
      "target_weight": 0.1057,
      "rationale": "Unchanged. Health ETF with a narrow interval (4.9%) and positive 1d/5d returns; +2.68pp above baseline retained as a stable core holding.",
      "confidence": 0.5
    },
    {
      "symbol": "A08",
      "target_weight": 0.0749,
      "rationale": "Unchanged, ~baseline (7.07%). 20d return -6.1% argues against adding despite moderate width (5.5%).",
      "confidence": 0.5
    },
    {
      "symbol": "A09",
      "target_weight": 0.115,
      "rationale": "Trim from 17.3% to 11.5%. Bonds are negative across 1d/5d/20d (-1.3%/-2.9%/-4.3%) — a rate-driven selloff, not flight-to-safety — and the 5d interval is symmetric around a near-zero median (no edge). Keeps defensive ballast ~2.5pp above baseline while cutting single-ETF concentration; main funding source for the rebalance.",
      "confidence": 0.55
    },
    {
      "symbol": "A05",
      "target_weight": 0.04,
      "rationale": "Add from 2.0% to 4.0%, still below baseline (5.08%). Gold is the diversifier that matters in a regime where both equities and bonds fell over 20d; wide interval (7.6%) keeps it below baseline.",
      "confidence": 0.53
    },
    {
      "symbol": "A01",
      "target_weight": 0.03,
      "rationale": "Initiate 3%, below baseline (3.53%). Restores single-stock diversification; widest interval (11.0%) and 44% annualized vol justify staying below baseline. Tech group total 11.2% vs 30% cap.",
      "confidence": 0.52
    },
    {
      "symbol": "A15",
      "target_weight": 0.05,
      "rationale": "Add from 3.0% toward baseline (5.09%). Financials stock with moderate width (7.6%); pairs with A08 without breaching the 30% group cap (12.5% total).",
      "confidence": 0.52
    },
    {
      "symbol": "A13",
      "target_weight": 0.02,
      "rationale": "Initiate 2%, well below baseline (4.17%). Energy stock width 9.3% — small diversifier only, sized conservatively given the uncertainty.",
      "confidence": 0.51
    },
    {
      "symbol": "A07",
      "target_weight": 0.02,
      "rationale": "Initiate 2%, well below baseline (3.73%). Health stock width 10.4% and slightly negative 5d median — smallest of the stock adds.",
      "confidence": 0.51
    },
    {
      "symbol": "A14",
      "target_weight": 0.0,
      "rationale": "Stay at zero. Tech exposure is already carried by A11 + A01; A14's 5d median is ~0, width 10.1%, and 20d return -4.2% make it the least attractive stock here.",
      "confidence": 0.5
    }
  ],
